Vim 9.1.0900: keep expired input checks nonblocking

inchar_loop() subtracts elapsed wall time before its first input check.
When a zero or finite timeout expires during that setup, the negative
result reaches RealWaitForChar(), which interprets it as an infinite
wait. On a preempted or slower host this stalls startup and insert-mode
redraw until another key arrives. This is an upstream timing defect,
not a Kandelo readiness or clock workaround. Clamp calculated expired
waits while retaining the explicit indefinite-wait branch.

Upstream source: https://github.com/vim/vim/blob/v9.1.0900/src/ui.c

--- a/src/ui.c
+++ b/src/ui.c
@@ -355,6 +355,12 @@
 		before_blocking();
 		continue;
 	    }
+
+	    // The first availability check must still run after its deadline.
+	    // A negative elapsed timeout means "expired", not "wait forever".
+	    // The deliberate infinite wait above bypasses this calculation.
+	    if (wait_time < 0)
+		wait_time = 0;
 	}
 
 #ifdef FEAT_JOB_CHANNEL
